# Copy this file to server/.env and fill in the values. server/.env is never committed and never shown in the app.
# The helpdesk runs without any of this: it starts on a built-in demo mailbox.

# --- Read the real shared mailbox in Microsoft 365 (Outlook) ---
# HELPDESK_PROVIDER=graph   (uncomment once the three Graph values below are filled in)
HELPDESK_MAILBOX=helpdesk@autostrad.com
GRAPH_TENANT_ID=
GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- What happens to an approved reply ---
# draft_only          saves it in the Outlook Drafts folder, a person presses Send (safest, the default for a real mailbox)
# send_after_approval sends it from the support mailbox the moment a person approves it
# HELPDESK_SEND_MODE=draft_only

# --- Contract, customer and invoice data (Orion Fleet, the CNR Portal): see CUSTOMER_API.md ---
# Without this the helpdesk reads the CRM's own demo records. The file config/customer-systems.json says which questions to ask and how to read the answers
# (it holds NO secrets); the lines below are only the addresses and READ-ONLY keys that file names. Try it with the stand-in systems: npm run dev:live
# HELPDESK_ACCOUNT_SOURCE=api       api = the customer systems, crm = the CRM's own records. Unset: the file decides ("enabled": true/false)
# CUSTOMER_SYSTEMS_CONFIG=config/customer-systems.json
# ORION_BASE_URL=https://orion.example.com/api/v2
# ORION_API_KEY=
# CNR_BASE_URL=https://cnr.example.com/api
# CNR_API_KEY=
# The real data: the CNR Portal's read-only CRM API, which reads the Fleet database. These three lines are all it takes:
# CUSTOMER_SYSTEMS_CONFIG=config/customer-systems.cnr.json
# CNR_BASE_URL=https://cnr-portal.example.com/api/crm/v1
# CNR_API_KEY=                      one of the keys in the portal's CRM_API_KEYS

# --- Optional AI layer (adds accuracy on messy emails; the helpdesk works well without it) ---
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# off | fallback (only when the rules are unsure) | always
# HELPDESK_LLM_MODE=always
# HELPDESK_LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5

# --- Server (production) ---
# NODE_ENV=production     turns on the safeguards: refuses to start with a weak JWT_SECRET, refuses to run the demo seed
# JWT_SECRET=             at least 32 random characters, e.g. the output of: openssl rand -hex 32
# API_PORT=4000
# API_HOST=127.0.0.1      listen on this address only (use it when a reverse proxy on the same machine is the only way in)
# CORS_ORIGIN=            leave empty behind a reverse proxy (production then allows no cross-origin calls); otherwise a comma list of origins
# ARC_DB_PATH=            where the SQLite database lives (default server/data/autostrad.db): local disk, not NFS or SMB
